{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>One witness.</b> This is a foundational principle — every witness in the Torah is two, unless it specifies for you one (Sanhedrin 30)." "<b>For every iniquity and for every sin.</b> To have his fellow punished on his testimony — not corporal punishment and not monetary punishment, but it establishes an oath. He said to his fellow: 'Give me a maneh that I lent you'; he said to him: 'You have nothing in my hand'; and one witness testifies for him that he does — he is liable to swear to him (Shevuot 40)." "<b>On the mouth of two witnesses.</b> And not that they write their testimony in a letter and send it to the court, and not that an interpreter stand between the witnesses and the judges (Sifrei)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:42.557847368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}